   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law, in relation to procurement lobbying; and to amend chapter 1 of the laws of 2005 amending the state finance law and other laws relating to restricting contacts in the procurement process and the recording of contacts relating thereto, in relation to extending the effectiveness of certain provisions thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "A9949",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A9949-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "ASSEMBLY",
       "districtCode": 18,
       "fullName": "Earlene Hooper",
       "imgName": "479_earlene_hooper.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 479,
       "sessionMemberId": 197,
       "sessionYear": 2009,
       "shortName": "HOOPER"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1-c, Leg L; amd §§139-j & 139-k, St Fin L; amd §16, Chap 1 of 2005",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A9949\n\nTITLE  OF  BILL:   An act to amend the state finance law, in relation to\nprocurement lobbying; and to amend chapter 1 of the laws of 2005  amend-\ning  the  state  finance  law  and  other  laws  relating to restricting\ncontacts in the procurement process and the recording of contacts relat-\ning thereto, in relation  to  extending  the  effectiveness  of  certain\nprovisions thereof\n\nPURPOSE: This bill makes improvements to and clarifies the extent of the\nprocurement  lobbying  law,  and extends the expiration date of that law\nfrom March 10, 2010 to July 31, 2014.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS: Section 1 of the bill  outlines  the  legislative\nintent of the initial procurement lobbying statute, including its legis-\nlative history.\n\nSection  2 amends State Finance Law (SFL) Section 139-j to add technical\nand substantive improvements, including a clearer definition of when the\nrestricted contact period begins and  the  responsibilities  of  govern-\nmental  agencies  during that period, as well as what communications are\nnot subject to the restricted contact provisions of the law.\n\nSection 3 amends SFL Section 139-k to  conform  its  provisions  to  the\namendments made to SFL Section 139-j in Section 2 of this bill.\n\nSection  4  amends section 16 of Chapter 1 of the Laws of 2005 to extend\nthe expiration date of SFL Sections 139-j.and 139-k to July 31, 2014.\n\nSection 5 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW: SFL §§ 139-j and 139-k set forth restrictions on  contacts\nduring  the  procurement  process  and.provide  for  recording  of  such\ncontacts. These statutes were originally subject to a December 31,  2007\nexpiration  date,  but  were extended to July 31, 2009 by Chapter 501 of\nthe laws of 2007 and to March 10, 2010 by Chapter 169 of 2009. These two\nstatutory provisions are set to expire on March 10, 2010.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: This is a new proposal.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT: Concerns about ensuring that the  public  procure-\nment  process is competitive, open and transparent, with a level playing\nfield for obtaining contract awards by participating  vendors,  resulted\nin  the  enactment  in 2005 of the procurement lobbying law set forth in\nSFL . 239-j and 139-k. These two provisions restrict contacts during the\nprocurement process and provide for  the  recording  of  such  contacts.\nWithout  an  extender,  these  requirements  would  be  eliminated. Such\nprovisions are  important  to  maintain  the  integrity  of  the  public\nprocurement  infrastructure  and  to continue with best practices in the\nestablishment and administration of State contracts.\n\nSince its enactment in 2005, these  provisions  have  greatly  increased\npublic  and  vendor  confidence  in the fairness and transparency of the\nState procurement process. However, concerns have  been  expressed  that\nthey  have  also  resulted  in the unintended consequences of creating a\nchilling   effect  on  legitimate  vendor-agency  communications  during\ngovernment procurements.\n\nSuch legitimate communications, as long as they  are  properly  recorded\nand evenhandedly received, are an important source of feedback for agen-\ncies  during  complex  procurements. The new provisions of this bill, in\nconjunction with its extension until 2014, would help to create  clarity\nand  certainty  for  both  vendors and governmental agencies as to their\nrights and responsibilities during the procurement process.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS: Minimal.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
