    "lawCode": "Amd §1-c, Leg L; amd §§139-j & 139-k, St Fin L; amd §16, Chap 1 of 2005",
    "lawSection": "Legislative Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A9949A\n\nTITLE  OF  BILL:    An  act  to  amend the legislative law and the state\nfinance law, in relation to procurement lobbying; and to amend chapter 1\nof the laws of 2005 amending the state finance law and other laws relat-\ning to restricting contacts in the procurement process and the recording\nof contacts relating thereto, in relation to extending the effectiveness\nof certain provisions thereof\n\nPURPOSE: This bill makes improvements to and clarifies the extent of the\nprocurement lobbying law, and extends the expiration date  of  that  law\nfrom March 10, 2010 to July 31, 2014.\n\nSUMMARY  OF  PROVISIONS:  Section 1 of the bill outlines the legislative\nintent of the initial procurement lobbying statute, including its legis-\nlative history.\n\nSection 2 amends Legislative Law Section 1-c to mirror changes.  in  the\nsame  definitions in State Finance Law (SFL) Sections 139-j and 139-k in\nSections 3 and 4 of this bill.\n\nSection 3 amends SFL Section 139-j  to  add  technical  and  substantive\nimprovements,  including  a  clearer  definition  of when the restricted\ncontact period begins and the responsibilities of governmental  agencies\nduring  that  period,  as well as what communications are not subject to\nthe restricted contact provisions of the law.\n\nSection 4 amends SFL Section 139-k to  conform  its  provisions  to  the\namendments made to SFL Section 139-j in Section 2 of this bill.\n\nSection  5  amends section 16 of Chapter 1 of the Laws of 2005 to extend\nthe expiration date of SFL Sections 139-j and 139-k to July 31, 2014.\n\nSection 6 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW: SFL SS 139-1 and 139-k set forth restrictions on  contacts\nduring  the  procurement  process  and  provide  for  recording  of such\ncontacts. These statutes were originally subject to a December 31,  2007\nexpiration  date,  but  were extended to July 31, 2009 by Chapter 501 of\nthe laws of 2007 and to March 10, 2010 by Chapter 169 of 2009. These two\nstatutory provisions are set to expire on March 10, 2010.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: This is a new proposal.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT: Concerns about ensuring that the  public  procure-\nment  process is competitive, open and transparent, with a level playing\nfield for obtaining contract awards by participating  vendors,  resulted\nin  the  enactment  in 2005 of the procurement lobbying law set forth in\nSFL S 139-j and 139-k. These two provisions restrict contacts during the\nprocurement process and provide for  the  recording  of  such  contacts.\nWithout  an  extender,  these  requirements  would  be  eliminated. Such\nprovisions are  important  to  maintain  the  integrity  of  the  public\n\nprocurement  infrastructure  and  to continue with best practices in the\nestablishment and administration of State contracts.\n\nSince  its  enactment  in  2005, these provisions have greatly increased\npublic and vendor confidence in the fairness  and  transparency  of  the\nState..  procurement process. However, concerns have been expressed that\nthey have also resulted in the, unintended consequences  of  creating  a\nchilling   effect  on  legitimate  vendor-agency  communications  during\ngovernment procurements. Such legitimate communications, as long as they\nare properly recorded and evenhandedly received, are an important source\nof feedback for agencies during complex procurements. The new provisions\nof this bill, in conjunction with its extension until 2014,  would  help\nto  create clarity and certainty for both vendors and governmental agen-\ncies as to.their rights  and  responsibilities  during  the  procurement\nprocess.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS: Minimal.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
